the world economy poses us a riddle  these days crises sanctions dropping oil  prices and sluggish growth and at the  same time nonetheless  a large number of data a large number of  news that do spread optimism  oliver raka from deutsche bank research  is here with me to clarify things to  straighten out things a little bit mr  rocco let's have a look at the debix  first that represents the economic mood  in the four biggest economies in the  world what is the debix telling us the  dmx has fallen slightly in november  this has been largely due to moderation  of sentiment in the united states the  ism has fallen a bit but still on a high  level otherwise we did have a rise in  the efo index for example in november  but this came after six declines and so  far we are still cautious to see this as  a turnaround necessarily  but all told even japan and also china  have seen  robust readings but all told still  the dbx as well as the individual  indicators point to moderate growth at  best unfortunately moderate growth  the stock markets don't really  seem to care about that very much the  ducks was down at 8 400 just about six  weeks ago is up at 10 000 points now  similar story with uh with the dow jones  in in new york  investors um seem to be in an excellent  mood there how do you explain that stock  market rush  that's due to several factors the most  important factors are definitely  central banks  and better growth data lately for one  there was a discussion about the united  states  and their central bank already rising  raising their interest rate early 2015  this expectation has been slow towards  mid-2015 by now and so far  the stimulus is here to stay longer in  the us and in europe we've gotten  increasing expectations and hopes  definitely for the ecb to do even more  for example doing public qe purchases of  government bonds all this added to each  other and also we had kind of a growth  scale in the course of q3  with weak growth numbers especially in  germany  this has  weakened sentiment on the financial  markets but the latest data has been  more stable partly positive and so far  the ducks bounce back  we will talk about the world economy  these days of course we have to talk  about the oil prices oil price is down  is still dropping  basically in free fall christine lagarde  the imf  managing director is saying that a low  oil price is not even such a bad  thing for the world economy how do you  see that that's um we agree  we think that's definitely a growth  upside for next year  because it increases the purchasing  power of households lowers cost basis  for a lot of companies  and so far  that might well be one of the positive  factors going into next year  on the other hand you always have to  keep in mind that a lot of countries are  all exporters and these might restrict  their consumption their imports and  especially their investment part of that  could be in the united states where  shale  oil shale gas have been an important  driver of investment lately if this  crumbles now  due to lower oil prices this might have  at least moderating effect on the  positive  real income effect  with the eurozone with eurozone or  europe's growth we need to be a little  more patient  that is true we have seen an increase in  growth finally positive growth numbers  middle of last year  but growth has been weak fragile uneven  as mario draghi keeps on saying and it's  definitely true  there is no clear growth path ahead for  europe yet  some countries have found their  turnaround spain portugal greece all of  them started growing again but italy and  france are definitely lagging behind  currently and so far  we are not really seeing much better  growth prospects in europe next year it  has indeed been quite an eventful year  for europe's economy in our focus  interview this month we want to recap  and to look back on europe's economic  year 2014  you can find that online on dw de slash  english slash mage in germany
 

